<!--
  /ssr — server-side rendering + client hydration.

  Grounded in the runtime: @ship/sui-runtime/server (server.ts) is the V8
  render entry; hydrate-mark.ts stamps the marker contract; mount.ts adopts
  the SSR markup (morph) and runs the compiled module's hydrate().
-->
<column class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Core concepts</p>
  <h1>SSR &amp; hydration</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A SUI view renders to HTML on the server for instant first paint, then the
    client re-attaches reactivity on top of that same markup — no re-render, no
    blank flash. One template, one set of bindings, two places they run.
  </p>

  <h2>One render, two runtimes</h2>
  <p>
    There is no second string-renderer. The server build aliases
    <code>@ship/sui-runtime</code> to its <code>/server</code> entry, which
    installs a minimal server DOM as <code>globalThis.document</code> and runs
    the <em>same</em> <code>bindText</code> / <code>bindAttr</code> /
    <code>bindIf</code> / <code>bindFor</code> binders the browser runs. Because
    the binders only touch the node they are handed, the identical render walks
    the server DOM and serializes to first-paint HTML — one isomorphic render
    artifact, not two code paths that can drift.
  </p>

  <h2>What the server produces</h2>
  <p>
    The serialized HTML is not inert. Each dynamic position carries a hydration
    marker so the client knows where reactivity re-attaches: dynamic text is
    wrapped in <code>&lt;!--sui:text:bN--&gt;…&lt;!--/sui--&gt;</code>, event
    handlers become <code>on:event="bN"</code>, and control-flow boundaries
    become <code>&lt;sui-if key="bN"&gt;</code> wrappers. The
    <code>markHydrateText</code> / <code>markHydrateAttr</code> /
    <code>markHydrateBoundary</code> helpers stamp these onto the server DOM;
    on the browser DOM they are true no-ops.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.markers}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>What the client does</h2>
  <p>
    Boot calls <code>mount(target, payload)</code>. When the target already
    holds server markup, mount does not overwrite it — it <code>morph</code>es
    the DOM in place toward the payload's HTML, preserving node identity, focus,
    scroll, and custom-element state (overwriting via <code>innerHTML</code>
    would discard the SSR content and cause a blank flash). With no server
    markup, it clean-mounts instead: the compiled view's <code>__mount</code>
    builds the DOM fresh.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way, mount then imports the compiled module, runs its
    <code>setup(ctx)</code>, and calls the module's <code>hydrate(root, ctx)</code>.
    That is the step that reads the markers above and wires the live bindings
    back onto the existing nodes. <code>hydrate</code> returns a disposer, which
    mount registers alongside the rest of the view's cleanups.
  </p>

  <h2>Server or client?</h2>
  <p>
    <code>scope()</code> is isomorphic. It runs on the server to produce the
    HTML and again on the client to hydrate it, so it must stay pure — reaching
    for <code>document</code> or <code>window</code> there throws in the V8
    render isolate that has neither. Anything browser-only belongs in
    <code>mount()</code>, which runs only on the client, after the DOM exists.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.guard}></sui-codeblock>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Guard by placement, not by feature-detection.</strong> You rarely
    need a <code>typeof window</code> check — put pure derivation in
    <code>scope()</code> and browser side effects in <code>mount()</code>, and
    the split falls out for free. The server never runs <code>mount()</code>.
  </p>

  <row class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/data">← Live data</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/motion">Motion →</a>
  </row>
</column>
